"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.8. The surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT2c, with positive surgical margins and lymphovascular invasion, but no seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of lymph node removal and missing nodal sampling widen the uncertainty regarding nodal status. The discrepancy between the biopsy and prostatectomy findings is noted, as the biopsy Gleason pattern and ISUP report are missing.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of extraprostatic extension and no seminal vesicle invasion. The postoperative PSA trajectory and subsequent follow-up are unknown, which significantly limits the certainty of the prognosis. Missing previous notes also impede a complete assessment. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The fixed estimated horizon is 59.86 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 35.91–83.80 months; its coverage has not been validated."
